 #### City Plots (AI)
 
 - Fix K-Mod some allocation pathologies: e.g. starving/stagnant cities no longer choose **+1 hammer** over **+4 food** unworked plots; also fix the "food is production" valuation so settlers/workers don’t undervalue food. See [KI#34](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#34---seemingly-fixedtweaked-major-k-mod-suboptimal-food-plot-ai-city-allocation-starving-or-stagnant-cities-prefer-1-hammer-over-4-food-plots-that-are-unallocated-to-any-population-ulundi-screenshots-prague-screenshots-and-others--food-not-being-valued-when-food-is-production-eg-worker-settler-etc-if-any-more-when-it-is-in-fact-the-exact-same-and-probably-more-reliable-to-set-rather-than-production).
-- (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 5996+) Update - **Refine the AdvCiv-SAS starvation guard to permit partial recovery:** Base AdvCiv blocked a job swap from creating starvation but, once a city was already starving, could allow a swap that worsened its food deficit. An earlier AdvCiv-SAS correction prevented those worsening swaps, but was too strict in the other direction: it rejected every reassignment that left any deficit, even when switching one population member to a higher-food plot substantially reduced starvation. The AI now blocks only food-reducing swaps while a deficit remains; equal-food improvements and partial food recovery can proceed when the full city-aware comparison approves them, while forced and free specialists remain protected. Map-428 BBAI logging had shown Bursa retain 4-5 Artists while starving by 5-7 food because its available 4-food plot could not eliminate the whole deficit in one reassignment. Across directionally comparable but diverging runs through turn 369, positive Artist-to-plot opportunities while starving fell from 144 to 84, positive opportunities with at least 4 Artists fell from 50 to 9, and the largest missed value fell from about 197,000 to 61,000; the severe Bursa and Rome cases disappeared.
+- (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 5996+) Update - **Refine the AdvCiv-SAS starvation guard to permit partial recovery:** Base AdvCiv blocked a job swap from creating starvation but, once a city was already starving, could allow a swap that worsened its food deficit. An earlier AdvCiv-SAS correction prevented those worsening swaps, but was too strict in the other direction: it rejected every reassignment that left any deficit, even when switching one population member to a higher-food plot substantially reduced starvation. The AI now blocks only food-reducing swaps while a deficit remains; equal-food improvements and partial food recovery can proceed when the full city-aware comparison approves them, while forced and free specialists remain protected. Map-428 BBAI logging had shown Bursa retain 4-5 Artists while starving by 5-7 food because its available 4-food plot could not eliminate the whole deficit in one reassignment; confirming runs no longer reproduced the severe Bursa and Rome starvation cases.
 - If happiness room is **high**, push growth harder: favor food tiles more, deemphasize hammers (unless **food is currently production**). See [KI#40](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#40---seemingly-fixed--addressed-tremendousmajor-plot-allocation-improvement-2-if-we-are-high-in-happiness-favour-food-tiles-much-more-and-hammer-tiles-much-less-if-happiness-is-really-high-favour-growth-dynamically-and-plot-allocation-of-these-the-more-we-can-afford-it-even-if-production-is-slightly-reduced-short-term-as-a-result-it-auto-corrects-at-high-happiness-with-seemingly-much-stronger-ai-and-developped-cities-as-a-result-excluded-if-food-is-production-eg-worker-settler-etc-if-i-am-not-mistaken-but).
 - (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 5927+) Update: now mitigated/halted by **high food loss from unhealthiness**, where it is inefficient to grow more (e.g., York size 25 with 25 health 36 unhealth (-11 food) and 39 happy 27 unhappy, +3 food per turn still, should not grow more and work specialists or other yields than food rather to increase efficiency).
 - (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 5989+) **Recheck and reassign citizens to safe, clearly better unworked plots:** K-Mod could keep a weaker worked (i.e. assigned to one population in city) plot because its quick initial estimate overlooked another plot that its full city-aware comparison considered better. If ordinary citizen allocation finds no change, the **AI now rechecks plots that improve at least one yield without reducing any**, **trade some production for a large food/commerce gain**, or otherwise **offer a substantial overall gain without losing food**. It switches **only when** the full city-aware valuation confirms the change is **beneficial**, never when the swap would cause starvation, and does not immediately reverse a plot assigned earlier in the same update. The newly added `SAS_BBAI_CITIZEN_LOG_LEVEL` BBAI logging test showed this corrected cases such as Karakorum working **2 food / 0 hammers / 1 commerce** instead of the strictly better **2 food / 1 hammer / 1 commerce** plot, made 624 approved swaps through turn 359 at normal game speed, and reduced remaining plot-to-plot contextual misses from 4,187 to 3,229 (about 23%); specialist-related swaps remain a separate target. (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 5990+) Note: for convenience, human cities use the same fallback by default (tunable) through `SAS_CONVENIENCE_HUMAN_CITIZEN_PLOT_FALLBACK_ENABLE`, but only while citizen automation is enabled; manual citizen assignments remain untouched.
 - (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 5995+) **Recheck modest food trades missed by K-Mod's quick plot estimate:** Additionally to the previous change, BBAI logging still found recurring high-value cases such as working **2 food / 1 hammer / 1 commerce** instead of **3 food / 0 hammers / 1 commerce**, or **3 food / 1 hammer / 1 commerce** instead of **4 food / 0 hammers / 1 commerce**. The fallback now also considers an unworked plot that provides at least **+1 food** while losing no more than **1 hammer and 2 commerce**, but reassigns the population only when the full city-aware comparison confirms that the trade is beneficial and the existing starvation/stability safeguards pass. Across directionally comparable but diverging map-428 BBAI runs through turn 348, this made 780 approved modest-food trades; unresolved records matching the new pattern fell from 1,460 to 0, all remaining plot-to-plot contextual-miss records fell from 3,702 to 2,404, and those valued above 1,000 fell from 762 to 162.
+- (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 5997+) **Consider additional beneficial food trades missed by K-Mod's quick plot estimate:** Additionally to the previous change, BBAI logging still found the preceding narrow limits could still exclude worthwhile changes such as **2 food / 0 hammers / 3 commerce** to **3 food / 0 hammers / 0 commerce**, even when the city's full valuation strongly preferred the extra food. The fallback now also checks any plot trade that gains at least **+1 food** without reducing the simple diagnostic score of **3 food + 2 hammers + 1 commerce**. This score only identifies candidates: the full city-aware comparison must still approve every reassignment, and the existing starvation and stability safeguards remain in force. In directionally comparable but diverging map-428 runs through turn 340, the new rule made 292 approved reassignments, eliminated all 372 previously unresolved records matching this pattern, reduced all remaining plot-to-plot misses from 2,434 to 1,936, and reduced those valued above 1,000 from 211 to 88; no new-rule reassignment was reversed within one turn.
 
